            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

We had identified that your house is pretty air tight already and there is an outside fresh air supply connected to each furnace. As the house requires fresh air (primarily when a kitchen range hood, bathroom fan or the clothes dryer is on), outside fresh air is brought in through those 2 fresh air supplies &amp; distributed through the ductwork system. You can adjust these fresh air vent openings ........ if there is an inline damper installed.



Although your house is currently quite air tight, it will likely become even tighter in conjunction with a couple of upgrades we talked about:



** Air leakage through the exterior wall plugs and switches should be reduced in conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade (including a full exterior Tyvek air barrier)



** The 7" chimney for the fireplace will be eliminated when you install a "direct vent" propane fireplace.



Savings: The software models a nominal 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.6 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates. For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4@16" with R12 insulation. We talked about your interest in obtaining some quotes to replace the exterior siding of your house and install new windows. This is a great time to add more exterior wall insulation.



Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.



For your house, I modeled the adding another R20 to 100% of the exterior walls of the house.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R20, you could potentially save $ 1426 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 36.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,940 is available for adding R20 insulation to 100% of the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The crawlspace below your house has R6 semi-rigid insulation on the interior of the concrete walls and there is R12 batt insulation in the floor above. You mentioned the contractor who installed your 2 new furnaces had confirmed the furnace that heats the main floor also provides some heat into the crawlspace.



This foundation does experience seasonal dampness in the spring that you address by using a sump pump. I also see 2 foundation wall vents along the front foundation wall (not the 2 x new fresh air supply vents for the furnaces)  and I assume these can be manually opened/closed seasonally to help vent the foundation. Perhaps the two rear foundation windows were meant to have screens and be opened for "cross ventilation" ? I would personally install a ventilation exhaust fan in one of the 2 vents that can be turned on during the non-heating season to help ventilate &amp; remove excess moisture from the foundation.



Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 2 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($1,000 for adding R10 - R23, and $2,000 for adding R24+).



For your house, I modeled increasing the crawlspace wall insulation (including the perimeter header space above) up to a minimum R24 (perhaps spray foam) for a net R18 increase.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the crawlspace walls and header space above by R16, you could potentially save $ 403 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



If you do not complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you could still be eligible for up to $ 500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $1,352 is available for adding R16 insulation to 100% of the crawlspace walls and header area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. 100% of the crawlspace walls and header area of your home must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</foundation>
                <windows>

You have recently installed a new patio door in the upper level residential unit of your home (which we call a big window). The other 13 windows in your house (except for the 2 in the crawlspace) are triple pane and of varying ages (some are wood, some are vinyl, and the 2 in the crawlspace are very old wood windows). If you decide to move forward with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, this may be a good time to consider an upgrade to some or all of these windows. I recommend you install ENERGY STARÂ® qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all 13 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 86 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates (12 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality. See the Additional Comments section of this report where I talk about this further, including my comments on the "Passive Ventilation" system currently installed in your house.



I recommend that you consider having an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. I recommend you install one that is a minimum 75% efficient and has an ECM circulation motor to keep operation costs low.



Savings:

By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, this will increase the ventilation of your house (which is required), with an estimated $ 99 increase per year in energy costs, increasing your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for installing an HRV.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

The upper floor living unit has a wood-burning fireplace that has a 7" chimney and it draws combustion air from inside the house. As outlined in the "Additional Comments" section of this report, your house is quite air-tight and the operation of exhaust appliances (range hood, bathroom fans &amp; clothes dryer) is expected to draw combustion products from the fireplace back into the house. You are interested in installing a direct-vent propane fireplace (with a spark ignition rather than a fuel wasting pilot light).



Savings: By replacing the current wood-burning fireplace with a direct-vent propane fireplace (with a spark ignition), there is an estimated $ 140 decrease per year in energy costs, reducing your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for installing a propane fireplace.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front facing roof (East), directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce a maximum 3,391 kWh annually (a full shading analyses should be completed by the company you source your system from), reducing your electricity bill by $ 1119, and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $14,500 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $8,000 for the wall insulation

     - $1,000 for the crawlspace wall &amp; header insulation

     - $3,000 for upgrading 12 windows with eligible ESTAR units

     - $   500 for a minimum10% air leakage reduction

     - $2,000 Bonus if you achieve a minimum 30% total energy reduction 



For more information, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the main floor range hood, 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 9.1 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment can be expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The fireplace in the upper floor unit is not a direct vent appliance &amp; is susceptible to back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke detectors and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood (main floor unit)

        - 2 bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer



NOTE: There is a dedicated make-up fresh air supply connected to each furnace, although I did not see an inline damper for you to manually adjust the amount of fresh air coming in.



If you choose to replace a bathroom fan, please choose an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified unit. For new bathroom fans, you may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs Â Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.40 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.25 per liter 

Propane - $1.00 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.99 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

